src/test/prolly/README

Requirements suite for prolly storage
=====================================

These tests encode what prolly storage must do: be true to Postgres (SQL,
concurrency and durability behave exactly as on heap) and true to Dolt
(branches, versioned schema, diff, merge, and commits whose root hash
depends only on their content).  Prolly meets its requirements when this
suite passes against it.  The suite
is not part of "make check" or "make check-world"; stock Postgres doesn't
run it.

Running
-------

	make -C src/test/prolly check

PROLLY_AM is the table access method under test (default prolly; heap
validates the tests, below).
Semantics, isolation and durability use a shared target setup and verify a
canary's AM before testing.  For prolly, public is a versioned schema, and
the canary's table, index and TOAST storage must have block-map forks.
Extensions live in an ordinary extensions schema.  Individual groups:

	check-semantics       SQL behaves exactly as on heap
	check-target          guard rejects accidental fallback to heap
	check-vc-target       commits, branches and restores the actual AM's data
	check-vc-target-guard rejects disconnected VC paths and no-op functions
	check-isolation       concurrency behaves exactly as on heap
	check-tap-durability  crash recovery, standby replay, prepared xacts
	check-vc              branches, versioned schema, diff, merge, history
	                      independence and canonical form of branches made
	                      by copying (always on prolly, whatever PROLLY_AM is)
	check-vc-isolation    sessions on different branches at once (likewise)
	check-tap-vc          history independence across clusters

Semantics, isolation and TAP groups require --enable-tap-tests; a build
without TAP support fails these targets instead of reporting success.

Run check-vc-target before claiming version-control coverage for an AM:

	make -C src/test/prolly check-vc-target

This strict probe checks actual table AMs, branch heads and commit parents,
insert/update/delete isolation in both directions, and hard reset to HEAD
and an older commit. It verifies full row contents, including large text,
using new connections so SQL writes are committed before history snapshots.
It uses a disposable cluster and leaves its fixtures there for diagnosis.
An unsupported or disconnected VC path fails; a successful dolt_* call or
an empty history is not evidence of coverage. Prolly passes; heap does
not. check-vc and check-vc-isolation run the probe
on prolly before their tests. This is a data targeting canary, not a claim
of schema, merge or crash-recovery coverage.

check-tap-vc requires check-vc-target before running. CI runs the probe
on prolly, and check-vc-target-guard. The latter proves that the
same probe rejects heap, plus deliberately ineffective commit, checkout
and reset functions. It checks the specific failing invariant, not
arbitrary SQL errors. These are negative controls for the harness, not
functional VC passes.

Postgres's own regression, isolation and recovery suites, run with every
user table in block-mapped storage (the storage under prolly's tables),
are a separate target, not part of "check":

	check-upstream-bmap

(prolly tables exist only in versioned schemas, which the upstream suites
don't make, so they run on block-mapped heap instead.)

PostGIS
-------

PostGIS isn't part of this tree, so its groups run only when given a
build of the pinned PostGIS (src/tools/prolly_bench/configs/postgis.toml)
made against this tree's installation prefix:

	python3 src/tools/prolly_bench/build_postgis.py \
	    --pg-config PREFIX/bin/pg_config --build-dir DIR
	make -C src/test/prolly check-postgis POSTGIS_BUILD=DIR

The build is installed from DIR into the temporary installation.  PostGIS
goes in public, versioned on prolly, as PostGIS's own tests expect.  The
groups:

	check-postgis-semantics  PostGIS on the AM under test answers as on
	                heap (t/006_postgis.pl, postgis_schedule; also run
	                by check-heap when POSTGIS_BUILD is set): a corpus of
	                every geometry type, dimension and kind of empty, NaN
	                and extreme coordinates, curves, TINs, values from
	                bytes to a megabyte, geography, rasters and spheroids
	                (gis_values); every PostGIS operator class, from the
	                catalog, with index and bitmap scans and KNN agreeing
	                with sequential scans, exclusion constraints,
	                statistics, estimated extents and plans (gis_indexes);
	                every type, operator class, operator and aggregate
	                PostGIS installs, with what covers it, operators and
	                aggregates evaluated on the stored corpus
	                (gis_catalog); metadata views and column management
	                (gis_metadata)
	check-postgis-isolation  concurrency as on heap (t/295_postgis_isolation.pl,
	                postgis_isolation_schedule; also run by check-heap):
	                SERIALIZABLE checks with spatial predicates, whose
	                GiST predicate locks fail one of two overlapping
	                inserts and neither of two far apart; an exclusion
	                constraint race; row locks on KNN results, waited for
	                and skipped
	check-postgis-durability  crash recovery (t/296_postgis_crash.pl, also
	                run by check-heap): committed geometries, large ones
	                out of line, survive a crash in a bulk load byte for
	                byte (EWKB digests), the load's don't, and the GiST
	                index agrees, under wal_consistency_checking; on
	                prolly, a crash in a commit of large geometries (in
	                its fold, and with its chunks reserved but not
	                committed) leaves the head and the working set as
	                they were
	check-vc-postgis  PostGIS through version control, on prolly
	                (vc_postgis_schedule): byte-identical values and
	                index agreement through checkout, reset, merge,
	                cherry-pick and revert, typmods kept and enforced
	                (vc_gis_moves); one root for one table built seven ways,
	                no change for storage-only rewrites, exact diffs,
	                merges of spatial edits and conflicts on other bytes,
	                geometry keys (vc_gis_history); types without binary
	                send/recv and NaN points (vc_gis_types); clone, push,
	                fetch and pull through a file remote (vc_gis_remotes);
	                out-of-database rasters, whose paths come back through
	                moves and a clone, and whose files aren't versioned
	                (vc_gis_outdb); ALTER EXTENSION postgis UPDATE with
	                committed and uncommitted data on two branches, and
	                DROP EXTENSION refused for another branch's columns
	                (vc_gis_extension)
	check-tap-vc-postgis  clone, push, fetch and pull through a network
	                remote, between two servers (t/297_postgis_network.pl)

gis_catalog's list changes when PostGIS adds a type, operator class,
operator or aggregate: the group fails until the new member is covered or
excluded there with a reason.

PostGIS's own regression suite runs separately, as it takes longer:

	make -C src/test/prolly check-postgis-regress POSTGIS_BUILD=DIR

t/299_postgis_regress.pl runs regress/run_test.pl, with PostGIS's own
hooks, for each suite (core, raster, topology, and sfcgal when built) in
each mode: heap; prolly (public versioned, and every table a test makes
in it checked to be a prolly table as it is made); prolly_txn (every
transaction a Dolt commit); and heap_dump and prolly_dump (--dumprestore:
the database dumped and restored once PostGIS is installed, before the
tests).  Each prolly mode's tests are compared with its heap baseline's,
by outcome and, where both failed, by their output: agree, regression,
postgres_semantics or known_bug (a difference listed in
postgis_regress_known with its issue and the md5 of its diff), crashed or
timeout.  A regression, crash or timeout fails the group, and so does a
listed test whose diff changed or that agrees.  Schemas a suite makes
itself (topology's, some tests') stay ordinary, so their tables are heap
beside the versioned public; the summary counts, per suite and mode, the
tables made on prolly and those made elsewhere.  The classification is in
tmp_check/postgis_regress/summary.txt.  POSTGIS_REGRESS_SUITES and
POSTGIS_REGRESS_MODES narrow a run, POSTGIS_REGRESS_TIMEOUT (seconds,
3600) bounds each.

Validating the tests themselves
-------------------------------

The semantics, isolation and durability groups describe Postgres
behaviour, so their expected output is what heap produces, and they must
pass against heap:

	make -C src/test/prolly check-heap

Run this whenever those tests change.  Their expected files are generated
from heap and then reviewed; never edit them to match an engine.

The version-control groups have hand-written expected output: each check
prints one "label|value" line.  They can't run against heap.

What is and isn't covered
-------------------------

Covered: requirements that are decided.

	Semantics       types, large values, COPY, TRUNCATE, DDL on data
	                (sem_basic, sem_ddl); every index AM on engine tables
	                (sem_indexes); savepoints, Halloween protection, ON
	                CONFLICT, cursors, ctid, xmin as a change token, 2PC
	                (sem_transactions); constraints and triggers
	                (sem_constraints); VACUUM, ANALYZE, CLUSTER,
	                partitioning, inheritance, materialized views
	                (sem_maintenance)
	Concurrency     row locks, unique waits, REPEATABLE READ conflicts,
	                SERIALIZABLE write skew, FK key-share locks, READ
	                COMMITTED re-checks, deadlocks (iso_*)
	Durability      crash recovery and prepared transactions (101),
	                standby replay and promotion (102), databases
	                copied from templates and dropped (103), all under
	                wal_consistency_checking
	Version control branches isolate data (vc_branches); schema is
	                versioned, including cached plans and functions
	                (vc_schema); diff and non-conflicting merge
	                (vc_diff_merge); sessions on different branches
	                (vc_branch_sessions)
	Canonical form  history independence within a database (vc_history),
	                across client encodings and databases (vc_canonical)
	                and across clusters (201)

Not covered until decided: temporary and unlogged tables, tablespaces,
logical decoding (deferred), the staging API, how merge conflicts are
reported, merges that change schema, and branch scope of cluster-wide
catalogs.  Clone, fetch and push are covered by test_prolly (its remote
tests, file and network) and timed by prolly_bench's V4.

The version-control tests assume the default branch is named "main" and
that an unknown branch is reported as
	prolly branch "NAME" does not exist

Bugs
----

Every bug found in prolly storage gets at least one test that fails without
the fix: here if it is a requirement-level behaviour, otherwise in
src/test/modules/test_prolly.
